[{"id":"VRCE-5-9-1","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":9,"verse":1,"english":"Then resigning that line of thought, that mighty monkey, regaining his equanimity, set out in another direction of thought touching Sītā.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-11-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5009001","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"de735694ec054aebee57c5dbed829264452a8343d18dae50e4bc7266b4006180","compact":"16bd4053fa2bbeb3872a68b982aae3bd3aa1de2daa1eb8659bce645d098bdd7d","nasal":"37777f4ffd2958c2c2af725bd33371cda43bce161d76f059f0405b57aea7143c"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-9-2","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":9,"verse":2,"english":"Separated from Rāma, that lady is incapable of sleeping, or eating, or decorating (her person), or drinking.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-11-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5009002","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dfefc2bee44bc6b08b4de0acf9add26eb55f14c1d045df6e2f4507d62a165333","compact":"de5f69e65d10ffed4beeccc181838aae2a5c8983745b30e726c5c6a6c4ee428f","nasal":"de5f69e65d10ffed4beeccc181838aae2a5c8983745b30e726c5c6a6c4ee428f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-9-3","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":9,"verse":3,"english":"She is incapable of associating with any other person, although he were the sovereign of the celestials himself. And even in all heaven there is none who is equal to Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.663,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-11-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5009003","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"212bd8dd5b1495107f7db3445c8c0c658ba5bde5a65900bcf5b2875b5dae2ccd","compact":"f612392d4db87779ac4663e7dd21f1f48327bdf42ea83ac380e33188ae8b8c61","nasal":"f612392d4db87779ac4663e7dd21f1f48327bdf42ea83ac380e33188ae8b8c61"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-9-4","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":9,"verse":4,"english":"Some were tired out with sporting; some with singing; some with dancing; and some were fast asleep through intoxication.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.758,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-11-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5009004","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"746bd9aa1c36e0abc7b60f1468a66a371da5f36befa473dd347ed134a9d656b0","compact":"de4349d223d752bcc986538cd9ee348cc37737bacd54d701e25eff9adb6faeed","nasal":"bca0b852c3e8b154396a22b7dae8972c1e475c73b9e11d774e102c3319606bda"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-9-5","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":9,"verse":5,"english":"Some supported themselves on Murajas, Mşdangas, or Celikās; and some women were sleeping on excellent sheets. And that leader of monkey-bands saw (that place of drinking) filled with thousands of women, adorned with ornaments engaged in their sleep) in depicting each other's loveliness, and explaining the sense of songs; conversant with season and place; understanding the meanings of word chiming in with season and place;* and skilful at play. *All this, remarks Rāmānuja, appeared from their talk during sleep.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.754,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-11-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5009005","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"df343c836211e785900bde5ecef1ff9c5746273a684ffb5a34c00d335cec6d16","compact":"08e7ea53ff38b642aaed86743c510234ac904f0886445c70a82937dd77d60220","nasal":"bb22da9a667f31fc13d09f205790dc16c6ac7452ca3873ffdb7163b954ed1c75"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-9-6","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":9,"verse":6,"english":"Some supported themselves 